.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Benchmark artifacts ARE tracked — see .bench/README.md for what is in there and why.
# Three things are still excluded, each for its own reason:
#
# Third-party corpora. Every directory under accent/ is re-downloadable with
# scripts/fetch_accent_data.py, together they are ~98 MB, and the AESRC re-upload
# declares no licence at all — that script says so at the point of use, and "local
# internal eval only" is not a posture that survives being committed anywhere. The
# manifests and result JSONs beside them are kept, which is what makes a re-fetch
# comparable to what was measured before.
.bench/accent/*/
# Unreferenced scratch. Nothing under scripts/ reads a .npy; these are debug dumps of
# raw audio arrays left behind by ingest, one of them named after the person it came
# from. 19 MB, no reader, and regenerable from inbox/ if one is ever wanted again.
.bench/**/*.npy
.bench/**/*.bak
# Voice auditions. `voices.py --speak --wav` renders one WAV per installed voice so a
# voice can be chosen by ear; the set changes whenever Windows gains or loses one, and
# nothing reads them afterwards.
.bench/voices/
# A run marker: `send_check.py --live` deletes and rewrites it every time, so tracking
# it would mean a dirty worktree after every measurement. Its content is the word
# "landed".
.bench/send-check-console.txt
# The volunteer recordings. Tracked once — they are people, and irreplaceable — and
# reversed on 2026-08-01: a clip in git rides every clone and push forever, which is
# the wrong durability for somebody's voice. History was rewritten to take them out.
# The originals live in D:\dev\flow-recordings\ beside a pre-rewrite bundle, and
# .bench/README.md says how a fresh clone gets them back.
.bench/recorded/
# Editor state, not project content: pane layout and local UI preferences.
.obsidian/
# `scripts/shots.py` output. Regenerated in about a minute by the script that writes it,
# and a full walk is ~24 PNGs at desktop resolution — the kind of thing that is worth
# looking at once and never worth carrying in every clone forever. Point it at a tracked
# directory with --out when a particular set is worth keeping.
.shots/
